#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197949 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa6b152-86d3-5f96-8a94-57f86a17a84d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Over a Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "2a32501f-d0f2-5e59-b7cc-a6b459269f6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12357" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$\\mathbf F(x, y, z) = \\left\\langle -y, \\: x, \\: z+2 \\right\\rangle$,\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D$, the flux of a vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(-P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2x \\\\\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf F(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(x, y, g(x, y)) &= ⟨ - y, x, z + 2⟩ \\mid _{z = x^{2} + y^{2}} \\\\\n&= ⟨- y_(⏟)_(P), x_(⏟)_(Q), x^{2} + y^{2} + 2_(⏟)_(R)⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)⟨ - y, x, z + 2⟩ \\cdot dS &= ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(- (-y) \\cdot (2x) - (x) \\cdot (2y) + x^{2} + y^{2} + 2)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(2xy - 2xy + x^{2} + y^{2} + 2)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}(x^{2} + y^{2} + 2)dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[x^{2}y + \\frac{y^{3}}{3} + 2y]_{0}^{1}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{3} + 2)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(x^{2} + \\frac{7}{3})dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{x^{3}}{3} + \\frac{7x}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{7}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3486150-b3c2-5faf-99d5-b42ced350996" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "c69a0f41-5b8f-5fcd-b058-94df2d7984bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12383" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle z+2y, \\: 3y+z, \\: x -z\\right\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=-3x+2y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$. Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v$, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n$AAAAAAA$\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D$, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v$, our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: -3u+2v \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(u,v) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u}, \\frac{∂y}{∂u}, \\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3u + 2v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 0,-3⟩ \\\\\nr_{v}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v}, \\frac{∂y}{∂v}, \\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(v), \\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3u + 2v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3,-2, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(u, v)) &= ⟨z + 2y, 3y + z, x - z⟩ \\mid _{x = u, y = v, z =-3u + 2v} \\\\\n&= ⟨(-3u + 2v) + 2v, 3v + (-3u + 2v), u - (-3u + 2v)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3u + 4v,-3u + 5v, 4u - 2v⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(u, v)) \\cdot (r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′}) &= ⟨ - 3u + 4v,-3u + 5v, 4u - 2v⟩ \\cdot ⟨3,-2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= (-3u + 4v) \\cdot (3) + (-3u + 5v) \\cdot (-2) + (4u - 2v) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-9u + 12v + 6u - 10v + 4u - 2v \\\\\n&= u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)⟨z + 2y, 3y + z, x - z⟩ \\cdot dS &= ∬_(D)F(r(u, v)) \\cdot (r_{u}^{′} \\cdot r_{v}^{′})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)udA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{1}udvdu\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue}u}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd2fdb-4cae-5f0b-bea0-e845d51190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral Using a Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "b81eb0cb-a740-5e86-921c-4baa8c179e55" :question/math-academy-id "e-12387" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D$, the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n$\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\Bigg(\\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} + Q - \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\Bigg) \\: \\textrm{d}A$,\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive $\\mathbf j$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D$, the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y = h(x,z)$, it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,z) = \\left\\langle x, \\: h(x,z),\\: z \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} &= ⟨1, \\frac{∂h}{∂x}, 0⟩ \\\\\nr_{z}^{′} &= ⟨0, \\frac{∂h}{∂z}, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{z}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\\n1\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix}1 &= ⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},-1, \\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $\\mathbf j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_z \\times \\mathbf{r}'_x =-\\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_z = \\left \\langle -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}, \\: 1, \\: -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(D)F(r(x, z)) \\cdot (r_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{z}^{′})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)⟨P, Q, R⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - \\frac{∂h}{∂x}, 1,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x} + Q - R\\frac{∂h}{∂z})dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd2fdb-4cae-5f0b-bea0-e845d51190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral Using a Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d964bfe-bf39-57c8-b770-09b4df02494e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194094} {:answer/id #uuid "91cc1aa0-76fc-53f0-bad3-4598d4b95e18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P$ and $-Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194095} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe3224f-d55b-5a02-ad80-8b25ec001d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $-Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194096} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1212c0-4db5-5471-b6eb-9287a22a5b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194097} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb6b37a-bf30-57aa-93f7-4145e3459547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $P$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91cc1aa0-76fc-53f0-bad3-4598d4b95e18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P$ and $-Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194095} :answer-field/id #uuid "115f65e8-b327-5386-8f05-a31173973928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194093}] :question/id #uuid "b8aa780b-8e8f-52b7-b96a-66254bcb10c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161189" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x, y)$ for $(x, y) ∈ D$, the flux of the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨P, Q, R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)(A\\frac{∂g}{∂x} + A\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA$.\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"}]}
